isticated software tools  so then i realized that let me  transition to  a more bioinformatics role and i joined  another masters immediately after my  first in  in the university of guelph so i did a  masters  in bioinformatics and some of the  reasons  why i did that was because of the the  changing dynamics of the field  my passion for both biology and  programming  i did get into computer science at  carnegie mellon for my bachelors  but at the time i was more interested in  biology  and i never really knew much about  bioinformatics or computational biology  so i because of that i got exposed to  different disciplines such as statistics  uh mathematics engineering computer  science  something that a person who just did  biology might not generally be exposed  to  so it did give me a lot of variety and i  didn't enjoy that so it was very  and i still enjoy it  so after doing my master's in  in bioinformatics um i i came across  i met ilya through one of his uh  interesting webinars and i he was  talking about his  bioinformatics for precision oncology  program and  we were discussing about the different  aspects  and um i i had the opportunity to  be one of the mentors for the program  i'm currently doing a teaching  and training of adult certificate  expected in august  so i do have the formal teaching skills  to embark in such a program and and it  was very  it was a very nice experience because i  have  i have the interest for cancer research  and it was very enlightening to see my  students with their own research  coming from a more wet lab approach  which is something i'm familiar with so  it was a very  i had the opportunity to talk to  research what lab research scientists  give them my  insights use the resources that we have  to  help improve their research by  incorporating important data science  techniques but for this program it was  particularly data science techniques for  cancer research  but the program that we're  talking about is more the imp